<StudioLayout title="The studio \u2014 Strategic Sloth" description="Meet Strategic Sloth, an independent studio founded by game UI and UX designer Remi Couture.">
<section class="page-intro container"><p class="eyebrow">THE STUDIO</p><h1>Thoughtful tools.<br>Playful possibilities.</h1><p class="lede">An independent tools and game studio bringing nearly 20 years of game interface experience to the next generation of creators.</p></section><section class="section container compact"><div class="about-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">FOUNDED BY REMI COUTURE</p><h2>Built from the work.<br>Made for what’s next.</h2><p>I founded Strategic Sloth to build the tools I kept wishing existed during production. After nearly 20 years designing game UI and UX, I know how much time disappears into rebuilding states, preparing assets, and keeping a design system consistent.</p><p>My work spans mobile, console, PC, and interactive products. That experience now informs a focused studio practice: thoughtful interfaces, practical developer tools, and ideas built around play.</p><p>The aim is simple: spend less time repeating production tasks and more time making the creative decisions that reach the player.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/remicouture/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Connect on LinkedIn <span>↗</span></a></div><figure class="founder-portrait"><img src="/studio-images/remi-founder.png" alt="Remi Couture, founder of Strategic Sloth, in his studio" width="1051" height="1497"><figcaption><strong>Remi Couture</strong><span>Founder · Game UI / UX designer · Builder</span></figcaption></figure></div><div class="experience-strip"><div><strong>Nearly 20 years</strong><span>Game UI, UX &amp; product design</span></div><div><strong>From screen to engine</strong><span>Unity · Unreal · Godot · WebGL · Custom</span></div><div><strong>Founder-led</strong><span>Direct collaboration, from brief to handoff</span></div></div><div class="experience-note"><p class="eyebrow">REMI’S PAST CLIENT &amp; PARTNER EXPERIENCE</p><p>Disney <span>·</span> Sony <span>·</span> Riot Games <span>·</span> Tencent <span>·</span> Universal Studios <span>·</span> Hasbro <span>·</span> Twitch <span>·</span> AppLovin</p></div>
<section class="selected-work" id="selected-work"><div class="section-heading subsection"><div><p class="eyebrow">SELECTED STUDIO WORK</p><h2>Experience, put to work.</h2></div><p>Our own products turn familiar production problems into practical tools.</p></div><article class="case-study"><div class="case-study-image"><img src="/studio-images/sf-compose.webp" alt="SliceForge Compose showing a fantasy alchemy interface with reusable components" loading="lazy" width="1536" height="1024"></div><div class="case-study-copy"><p class="eyebrow">01 / GAME UI PRODUCTION</p><h3>SliceForge</h3><p>Take a visual reference beyond the flat image. Build editable layouts, prepare component states, and export a reusable UI kit.</p><dl><dt>The challenge</dt><dd>Turning a promising mockup into an interface a team can actually build.</dd><dt>The approach</dt><dd>Keep structure, states, preview, and export in one focused workflow.</dd></dl><a class="text-link" href="https://www.sliceforge.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Explore the product <span>↗</span></a></div></article><article class="case-study case-study-secondary"><div class="case-study-copy"><p class="eyebrow">02 / UNREAL ENGINE NAVIGATION</p><h3>Focusrail</h3><p>A UMG plugin for one of the details players feel immediately: reliable menu focus.</p><dl><dt>The challenge</dt><dd>Keeping controller navigation predictable across menus, popups, and scrolling screens.</dd><dt>The approach</dt><dd>Remember focus, track navigation paths, and bring the right widget into view.</dd></dl><a class="text-link" href="https://www.fab.com/listings/47a56c00-ab32-4c20-8aba-04935b4a2578" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View Focusrail on Fab <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="focus-flow" aria-label="Focusrail navigation behavior illustration"><span class="micro">NAVIGATION BEHAVIOR / ILLUSTRATION</span><div><span>01</span><strong>Open a menu</strong></div><i aria-hidden="true">↓</i><div><span>02</span><strong>Move between widgets</strong></div><i aria-hidden="true">↓</i><div class="flow-active"><span>03</span><strong>Return with focus intact</strong><b aria-hidden="true">↵</b></div></div></article><article class="case-study drive-case"><div class="case-study-copy"><p class="eyebrow">03 / DESKTOP WORKFLOWS</p><h3>DriveDeck</h3><span class="status launch">Built · Awaiting launch</span><p>An external SSD becomes a working environment: organize files, manage transfers, and safely eject when the work is done.</p><dl><dt>The challenge</dt><dd>Making a portable drive feel like a coherent workspace, from plugging in to disconnecting.</dd><dt>The approach</dt><dd>A familiar desktop surface backed by a transfer engine, recoverable file operations, and native safe eject.</dd></dl><a class="text-link" href="/drivedeck/">Explore DriveDeck <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="drive-case-art"><div class="drive-illustration" aria-hidden="true"><div class="drive-window"><div class="drive-window-bar"><strong>DriveDeck</strong><span>YOUR WORKSPACE, ON YOUR DRIVE</span></div><div class="drive-folders"><div><i></i>Projects</div><div><i></i>Archives</div><div><i></i>Music</div><div><i></i>DJ Sets</div></div><div class="drive-window-bottom"><span>Organize. Transfer. Take it with you.</span><b>↗</b></div></div><span class="image-label">WORKSPACE ILLUSTRATION · WINDOWS FIRST</span></div></div></article><article class="case-study narria-case"><div class="narria-case-art"><div class="narria-canvas" aria-hidden="true"><div class="narria-canvas-top"><strong>NarriaFlow</strong><span>A WORLD TAKING SHAPE</span></div><svg class="story-connections" viewBox="0 0 500 300" preserveAspectRatio="none"><path d="M110 100 C230 100 160 170 260 170 S350 70 415 70 M260 170 C310 170 330 250 420 240"/></svg><div class="story-note story-person"><span>CHARACTER</span><strong>The cartographer</strong><p>She remembers places<br>that no longer exist.</p><i>Connected to Chapter 01</i></div><div class="story-note story-chapter"><span>CHAPTER 01</span><strong>The city<br>between tides</strong><p>At dawn, the streets<br>began to disappear.</p><div class="story-lines"></div></div><div class="story-note story-world"><span>WORLD NOTE</span><strong>A changing coast</strong><div class="story-lines"></div></div><span class="narria-art-label">CANVAS ILLUSTRATION · SAMPLE STORY</span></div></div><div class="case-study-copy"><p class="eyebrow">04 / VISUAL WRITING</p><h3>NarriaFlow</h3><p>A book-writing web app for authors who think visually. An infinite canvas connects the outline, manuscript, characters, and ideas behind a world.</p><dl><dt>The challenge</dt><dd>Keeping a story’s structure and its many connections close to the writing.</dd><dt>The approach</dt><dd>Bring spatial planning, rich-text editing, linked organization, and AI story assistance into one workspace.</dd></dl><a class="text-link" href="/narriaflow/">Explore NarriaFlow <span>↗</span></a></div></article><article class="case-study world-case"><div class="case-study-copy"><p class="eyebrow">05 / WORLD AUTHORING</p><h3>SliceForge World Editor</h3><span class="status neutral">In development</span><p>A grid-based authoring surface in SliceForge Studio, with a working core that turns painted cells into a previewable, playable world.</p><dl><dt>The challenge</dt><dd>Keeping map authoring, generated scenes, and playtesting grounded in the same world data.</dd><dt>The approach</dt><dd>Use the painted grid as the source of truth, regenerate affected components, and stream visible chunks into the preview.</dd></dl><a class="text-link" href="/world-editor/">Explore World Editor <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="world-case-art"><img src="/studio-images/world-editor-diagram.svg" alt="Schematic world generated from a painted tile grid" width="600" height="430" loading="lazy"></div></article></section><div class="section-heading subsection"><div><p class="eyebrow">WHAT WE BRING</p><h2>Craft across the whole experience.</h2></div></div><div class="three-grid"><article class="plain-card"><span class="card-number">01</span><h3>Game UI & UX</h3><p>HUDs, menus, flows, and design systems that help players find their way.</p></article><article class="plain-card"><span class="card-number">02</span><h3>Developer tools</h3><p>Practical products that simplify repetitive work and support the creative process.</p></article><article class="plain-card"><span class="card-number">03</span><h3>Product direction</h3><p>Clear priorities, considered interactions, and a path from initial idea to engine handoff.</p></article></div></section><section class="contact-band"><div class="container"><div><p class="eyebrow">BUILD SOMETHING GOOD</p><h2>Your next idea.<br>Our kind of challenge.</h2></div><a class="button light" href="/contact/">Work with the studio <span>↗</span></a></div></section>
</StudioLayout>
